// error
// A condition broken BEFORE its operator (#1430). This is not a parser gap: §7
// inserts a `;` after `1` because insertion depends only on the last token of
// the line, and being inside an unclosed `(` does not suppress it.
//
// Suppressing it there would mean tracking bracket depth in the lexer — the
// newline-sensitivity §7 explicitly rules out ("one token of state ... no
// lookahead"), and the same reasoning that kept the wrapped `import` form
// rejected in #1412. So the form stays rejected and §7 documents the working
// spellings (trailing operator, or breaking after the `(`).
//
// What this case pins is the HINT: the offending `;` appears nowhere in the
// source, so without it the error names a token the reader cannot see.
fn main() {
  let a = 1
  let b = 2
  if (a == 1
      && b == 2) {
    print("unreachable\n")
  }
}
